    "answer": {
      "short": "$1\\binom n1+2\\binom n2+\\cdots+n\\binom nn=n2^{n-1}$.\n\nDifferentiate the binomial expansion, then substitute $x=1$.",
      "worked": "The binomial theorem gives\n$$\\begin{align*}(1+x)^n&=\\binom n0+\\binom n1x+\\binom n2x^2+\\cdots+\\binom nnx^n.\\end{align*}$$\nDifferentiating term by term,\n$$\\begin{align*}n(1+x)^{n-1}&=\\binom n1+2\\binom n2x+3\\binom n3x^2+\\cdots+n\\binom nnx^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$\nSubstitute $x=1$:\n$$\\begin{align*}1\\binom n1+2\\binom n2+3\\binom n3+\\cdots+n\\binom nn&=n(1+1)^{n-1}\\\\1\\binom n1+2\\binom n2+3\\binom n3+\\cdots+n\\binom nn&=n2^{n-1}.\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 60
  },
